        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Gray blacktop is usually a surface problem, and surface problems are what sealer fixes. Diagnose first: oxidation and light raveling are worth sealing, while alligator cracking and potholes need repair before any sealer goes down. Then buy a filled product, plan for more pails than the label suggests, and give yourself two dry days.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Brush the first coat in, squeegee the second across it, keep cars off for two days, and do not reseal again until the water test says so. Done that way, a few hundred dollars and a weekend of work can keep an aging driveway in service for years longer than it would last untouched.
          </p>
